**Read the table this way:** the rows that succeed fastest are the ones that change nothing. The row with the most research invested in it (native boot) is the one whose best case is a text-mode shell on a phone you already have a better version of.

**On "lightweight Linux userspace alongside Android" (option 2 in your brief):** this is essentially V0, and I think the plan underrates it. It deserves to be a first-class track with its own milestone, not a stepping stone to a "real" rootfs. A BusyBox + `dropbear` + `busybox httpd` static payload is a genuine Linux userspace. The chroot is a convenience (a real `/etc`, relative paths, per-project state) and a *later* refinement, not a prerequisite.

**On the ROM-swap option:** I want to flag it because the repo does not evaluate it, and because it is genuinely feasible — [androidarmv6 built a stable, daily-driver CM11 (Android 4.4.4) specifically for `samsung-totoro`](https://github.com/androidarmv6/android_device_samsung_totoro), with Wi-Fi, BT, GPS, camera and RIL fixed ([XDA dev thread](https://xdaforums.com/t/devs-only-cyanogenmod-11-for-samsung-bcm21553-development-discussion.2766229/)). That is real, device-specific, community-validated evidence — not a similar device. It is *not* a Linux environment, so it does not serve the north star directly, but it is a legitimate high-payoff option and a possible prerequisite for a better chroot experience (better terminal apps, more RAM headroom). It is a boot-critical write against a device whose EFS you cannot back up, so it belongs behind the full preservation gate, with an explicit "do we accept bricking this phone?" conversation. It is **not** the next step.

