# What I would validate first—and why

**Drop a statically linked ARMv6 `dropbear` on the Totoro and SSH into a root shell over Wi-Fi — after a ten-minute read-only check of free RAM and Wi-Fi state, and nothing else.**

Specifically: run E0.1 and E0.6, then E1, then E2. Nothing else.

**Why this one experiment, above all others:**

- **It is the shortest path to a genuinely useful result.** It converts a rooted 2011 phone into a real UNIX machine reachable over the air, in under an hour of work.
- **Its risk is essentially zero.** It writes one file to `/data/local/tmp` and starts a process. Rollback is `kill` and `rm`. It touches no partition, no `/system`, no boot chain, no EFS.
- **It carries the most information per minute.** It simultaneously proves: static ARMv6 binaries execute on this CPU; the SD card is a viable payload location; Wi-Fi association and a routable IP work from a root shell (an item your earlier capture left unexplained); the whole "does a real Linux program run here" question, which currently gates five separate roadmap items.
- **It de-risks everything downstream.** If a static hard-float ARMv6 binary runs from `/data/local/tmp`, it runs from a loop-mounted ext3 image on the SD card, and the chroot milestone becomes plumbing rather than a compatibility gamble. If it *fails*, you learn that in ten minutes on the host, not after a weekend of rootfs assembly.
- **It is the step the plan skipped.** The plan's first technical gate is an audit of an Alpine armhf binary — a payload carrying a real, documented kernel-version risk (musl wants ≥2.6.39; you are on 2.6.35.7). Starting with a static binary removes that risk from the critical path entirely and defers the distribution question until after you already have something you use every day.

Everything else in this review — the chroot, the web UI, the retro handheld, even the decision to close native boot — follows more easily once you can type into a real shell on the Totoro from your laptop. That is the shortest safe, reliable, verifiable path, and it starts with a single static binary.
